COMMIT c42887ae0c50ba6c622d9de4072667a450206db9
docs(core): takeLocked's loop now carries the invariant that makes it a loop

The outer while in BatchingWriter.takeLocked can only ever run once, and the
invariant that makes that true was written down nowhere. So a reader saw a loop
that looked capable of several batches per drain, and drainLocked's javadoc
described an ordering concern that follows from a multi-batch drain that cannot
happen. Comments only; no statement changed. D-112 records the decision to keep
the loop anyway.

The invariant, now in the javadoc and verified by the reviewer against enqueue,
flush, onWindow and afterCommit: at rest either the queue holds fewer than
batchSize or every commit slot is taken, and waiters exist only when the queue
is full, which implies every slot is taken. Entered with every slot taken the
condition is false at once. Entered with at most batchSize queued -- enqueue can
reach exactly batchSize for an instant -- the first pass takes the whole queue
and there are no waiters to refill it. Entered from afterCommit with a slot just
freed, the one batch retakes that slot, so the condition is false whatever the
waiters refilled. That last entry is the only path where waiters do refill, and
the first draft of the analysis left it out.

The first review FAILed on the rationale, which was mine. D-112 claimed an if
would leave items queued with no commit in flight and no window armed, "a silent
stall". That is false, and it was contradicted by a sentence four lines above the
block I was editing: drainLocked's own javadoc already says nothing stays queued
with no commit in flight and no window. A take always does inFlight++, and that
commit's completion re-enters afterCommit and drains the rest. An if would be
correct; it would cost latency, because leftovers wait for the next completion or
window. The while is kept for the latency, and D-112 now says so.

The surviving equivalent mutant -- the loop's repeat -- is recorded as
unmeasured rather than counted: PIT has no while-to-if mutator, so it may not be
a PIT mutant at all, and no PIT report has been run on this class. No test is
possible for a doc-only change; the packet argues that with FIX-129 as precedent
and the reviewer looked for a non-vacuous case and found none either.

A flake surfaced in the first full-suite run of this task and is now FIX-144,
with both runs kept as evidence. FIX-143's packet is added here.

