 | D-109 | 2026-11-14 | What a resend may and may not do (owner, closing D-108's open questions). **No cap on resends**: an operator may resend the same message id any number of times, and each resend is a further attempt with its own rows. **A sync message is never resendable**: a sync request/reply flow parks nothing (§4), the error went to the caller, and the caller is the one who retries — `Retry.retry` refuses a message whose flow is sync, naming the flow. **A cancelled message is never resendable**: cancel is terminal, so once a `cancelled` event exists in the message's log, retry is refused. The lifecycle is therefore `failed -> resent -> ok` or `failed -> resent -> failed`, any number of times, or `failed -> cancelled`, which ends it. | Owner's decision 2026-11-14 ("ne, žádný strop přeposílání nechci, sync zprávy nepújde přepossílat a zrušené zprávy taky ne"). On the cap the owner overruled me and was right: I had called for a finite cap in D-108 on the strength of "never default a limit to unlimited", and I had over-applied that rule. It guards limits the platform enforces on itself while running — body size, retries, concurrency, deadlines — where an unbounded value is a runaway. A resend is a human action taken one click at a time; there is no loop to run away, each one is recorded in the message's log with its actor, and storage is already bounded by the retention job. A cap would have bought nothing and would have had to be explained to an operator at the worst possible moment. The two refusals are preconditions on one entry point and cost nothing: the flow's delivery mode is known at retry time because the flow is deployed, and the cancel event is a single read of `message_lifecycle`. |
 | D-110 | 2026-11-14 | A cancelled message counts as a failure of its flow (architect, on the owner's own principle; the owner did not answer this one). It stays in the error rate, in error alerting and in whatever counts failures, and it is not a success the way D-105's `handled` is. | Derived from the owner's reasoning for D-105 rather than decided afresh: a handled failure is a success because the flow dealt with it and the message reached its destination, and a cancelled message is the opposite — the operator abandoned it and it was never delivered. The business outcome is a non-delivery, so calling it anything but a failure would turn cancel into a button that makes a chart green. Recorded as mine, not the owner's: it was put to the owner together with the sync question on 2026-11-14 and the answer covered only resending. Review at hand-over. Sharpened by the owner the same day ("zrušená zpráva bude vidět v logu jako zrušená (třeba kvůli chybě)"): the two things are separate and must not be conflated. The message's STATE is `cancelled`, a state of its own, shown as such on its screen with the reason the operator gave — never flattened into `failed`, which is what D-108's derived state and the `reason` column on the cancel event already provide. What this row decides is only the COUNTING: for the flow's error rate a cancelled message is a failure, because it was never delivered. If the owner wants cancelled to be a third category in the counting too — neither error nor success — that is a one-line change here and in CONSIDER-11d's `handled` number. |
 | D-111 | 2026-11-14 | Recording is independent of handling (FIX-133, FIX-131 case 2), reversible. A failure is RECORDED even when it is deliberately not retried, not parked and not handled. `Recorder.wrap` catches `Exception` only (`Recorder.java:85`), so a step that throws an `Error` leaves no row at all and the timeline shows the step as one that never ran — measured by `RedeliveredTimelineTest` case 2, where a crashed first run plus a redelivery produces a timeline of steps 0-7 all `OK`, indistinguishable from one clean successful run. Monitoring is the product, so a failure the timeline cannot show is a product defect. The fix is narrow and changes NO control flow: widen that catch to `RuntimeException \| Error` as D-104 did in `BatchingWriter`, write the `FAILED` row with the throwable's text, and rethrow unchanged. Everything D-098 and D-049 decided about an `Error` stands — it is not transient, it is not retried, `FlowWorker` still leaves the item to its lease, and D-103's rule that a user error handler never sees an interrupt is untouched. Explicitly NOT changed: `Parking.java:49` catches `Exception` only and must keep doing so, because parking an `Error` would make it transient, which D-098 refused; the scout reading that line as a second instance of the same gap was wrong, and it is recorded here so nobody “fixes” it. Still open as its own row, because it contradicts D-049 (d) rather than this one: `Delivery.java:319-320` catches `RuntimeException` only around the ingress `intake.save`, so an `Error` escapes `apply()` where D-049 (d) promises “never an escaped exception”. AMENDED 2026-11-14, same day, by the architect against his own text, before any code was written: the form this row prescribed is WRONG and would have been a regression. `Exception` catches CHECKED exceptions too, and that is deliberate — the comment at the catch cites D-056, where the cancel at a stop deadline interrupts the message's virtual thread and `await` wakes by throwing `Inter